[{"category":"section","location":"valuation/#Valuation","page":"Valuation","text":"","title":"Valuation"},{"category":"section","location":"valuation/#Perpetuity","page":"Valuation","text":"PV = fracCFr","title":"Perpetuity"},{"category":"section","location":"valuation/#Terminal-value","page":"Valuation","text":"Gordon growth model, applied to the free cash flow of the last forecast year:\n\nTV_n = fracFCF_n(1 + g)WACC - g","title":"Terminal value"},{"category":"section","location":"valuation/#Fade-stage","page":"Valuation","text":"A transition stage between the explicit forecast and the terminal value, in which growth declines from g_0 to g in linear increments over m years, reaching g in the last year of the stage:\n\ng_k = g_0 + (g - g_0)frackm qquad\nFCF_n+k = FCF_n+k-1(1 + g_k) qquad k = 1 dots m","title":"Fade stage"},{"category":"section","location":"valuation/#DCF-valuation","page":"Valuation","text":"The forecast free cash flows plus the terminal value, either with Gordon growth or with an exit multiple:\n\nEV = sum_t=1^n fracFCF_t(1 + WACC)^t + fracTV_n(1 + WACC)^n\nqquad TV_n = textMultiple times textEBITDA_n text(exit multiple)\n\nWith the mid-year convention the exponents become t - 05 (and n - 05 for a Gordon terminal value).","title":"DCF valuation"},{"category":"section","location":"valuation/#Sensitivity-table","page":"Valuation","text":"","title":"Sensitivity table"},{"category":"section","location":"valuation/#Equity-value","page":"Valuation","text":"textEquity = EV - textDebt + textCash - textMinority interest\n- textPreferred - textLeases + textNon-operating assets","title":"Equity value"},{"category":"function","location":"valuation/#CorporateFinance.perpetuity","page":"Valuation","text":"perpetuity(cashflow::Real, rate::Real)\n\nCalculates the present value of a constant perpetuity: cashflow / rate.\n\nArguments\n\ncashflow: Constant cash flow received each period.\nrate: Discount rate. Must be positive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorporateFinance.perpetuity"},{"category":"function","location":"valuation/#CorporateFinance.terminal_value","page":"Valuation","text":"terminal_value(; fcf::Real, wacc::Real, g::Real)\n\nCalculates the terminal value at the end of the forecast period using the Gordon growth model: fcf * (1 + g) / (wacc - g).\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nfcf: Free cash flow of the last forecast year.\nwacc: Discount rate. Must be greater than g.\ng: Long-term growth rate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorporateFinance.terminal_value"},{"category":"function","location":"valuation/#CorporateFinance.fade_forecast","page":"Valuation","text":"fade_forecast(; fcf::Real, g_start::Real, g::Real, years::Integer)\n\nExtends a forecast with a transition stage in which growth declines from g_start to the long-term rate g in linear increments. Returns years free cash flows, the growth rate of year k being g_start + (g - g_start) * k / years, so the last year of the stage already grows at g.\n\nFor example, g_start = 0.30, g = 0.05 and years = 5 give growth rates of 25%, 20%, 15%, 10% and 5%.\n\nAppend the result to the explicit forecast to build a three-stage DCF: dcf_valuation(vcat(fcf, fade_forecast(...)); wacc, g).\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nfcf: Free cash flow of the last explicit forecast year.\ng_start: Growth rate of the high-growth stage (the last explicit forecast year).\ng: Long-term growth rate, reached in the last year of the stage.\nyears: Length of the stage. Must be at least 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorporateFinance.fade_forecast"},{"category":"function","location":"valuation/#CorporateFinance.dcf_valuation","page":"Valuation","text":"dcf_valuation(fcf::AbstractVector{<:Real}; wacc::Real, g = nothing,\n              exit_multiple = nothing, ebitda = nothing, midyear::Bool = false)\n\nValues a company with a discounted cash flow model: the forecast free cash flows plus a terminal value after the last year. The terminal value is calculated either with the Gordon growth model (pass g) or with an exit multiple (pass exit_multiple and ebitda), but not both.\n\nWith midyear = true the cash flows are discounted at t - 0.5, as if they were received in the middle of each year. A Gordon terminal value is then also discounted at n - 0.5, while an exit-multiple terminal value stays at n, since it represents a sale price at the end of the last year.\n\nReturns a named tuple with pv_fcf, pv_terminal and enterprise_value.\n\nArguments\n\nfcf: Forecast free cash flows, one per year (year 1 to n).\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nwacc: Discount rate. Must be greater than -1, and greater than g if given.\ng: Long-term growth rate after the forecast period.\nexit_multiple: EV/EBITDA multiple applied at the end of the forecast. Cannot be negative.\nebitda: EBITDA of the last forecast year, used with exit_multiple.\nmidyear: Use the mid-year discounting convention. Defaults to false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorporateFinance.dcf_valuation"},{"category":"function","location":"valuation/#CorporateFinance.dcf_sensitivity","page":"Valuation","text":"dcf_sensitivity(fcf::AbstractVector{<:Real}; waccs::AbstractVector{<:Real},\n                gs::AbstractVector{<:Real}, midyear::Bool = false)\n\nBuilds the classic sensitivity table of a DCF: the enterprise value for each combination of discount rate (rows) and long-term growth rate (columns), using dcf_valuation with a Gordon terminal value.\n\nCombinations where wacc <= g have no valid Gordon value and are returned as NaN, so the rest of the table is still produced.\n\nArguments\n\nfcf: Forecast free cash flows, one per year (year 1 to n).\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nwaccs: Discount rates, one per row, in the same order.\ngs: Long-term growth rates, one per column, in the same order.\nmidyear: Use the mid-year discounting convention. Defaults to false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorporateFinance.dcf_sensitivity"},{"category":"function","location":"valuation/#CorporateFinance.equity_value","page":"Valuation","text":"equity_value(; ev::Real, debt::Real, cash::Real = 0, minority_interest::Real = 0,\n             preferred::Real = 0, leases::Real = 0, non_operating::Real = 0)\n\nCalculates the equity value from the enterprise value: ev - debt + cash - minority_interest - preferred - leases + non_operating.\n\nOnly subtract leases (IFRS 16 lease liabilities) if the free cash flows were calculated before lease payments; otherwise they would be counted twice.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nev: Enterprise value.\ndebt: Total financial debt.\ncash: Cash and equivalents.\nminority_interest: Non-controlling interests in consolidated subsidiaries.\npreferred: Preferred stock.\nleases: Lease liabilities.\nnon_operating: Non-operating assets (e.g. investments not included in the cash flows).\n\nAll arguments except ev cannot be negative and, except debt, default to 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorporateFinance.equity_value"},{"category":"section","location":"cost_of_capital/#Cost-of-capital","page":"Cost of capital","text":"","title":"Cost of capital"},{"category":"section","location":"cost_of_capital/#CAPM","page":"Cost of capital","text":"k_e = r_f + beta cdot ERP + CRP","title":"CAPM"},{"category":"section","location":"cost_of_capital/#WACC","page":"Cost of capital","text":"WACC = fracEE + D k_e + fracDE + D k_d (1 - t)","title":"WACC"},{"category":"section","location":"cost_of_capital/#Beta-regression","page":"Cost of capital","text":"beta = fracoperatornamecov(r_a r_m)operatornamevar(r_m)","title":"Beta regression"},{"category":"section","location":"cost_of_capital/#Unlevered-and-relevered-beta","page":"Cost of capital","text":"Hamada equation, extended with an optional debt beta beta_D (Conine). With beta_D = 0 (the default) it reduces to the classic Hamada formula.\n\nk = (1 - t) DE\nqquad\nbeta_U = fracbeta_L + beta_D k1 + k\nqquad\nbeta_L = beta_U + (beta_U - beta_D) k","title":"Unlevered and relevered beta"},{"category":"function","location":"cost_of_capital/#CorporateFinance.capm","page":"Cost of capital","text":"capm(; rf::Real, beta::Real, erp::Real, crp::Real)\n\nCalculates the Cost of Equity using the Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM).\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nrf: Risk-free rate. Can be negative.\nbeta: Systematic risk measure. Must be between -5 and 5.\nerp: Equity Risk Premium. Cannot be negative.\ncrp: Country Risk Premium. Cannot be negative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorporateFinance.capm"},{"category":"function","location":"cost_of_capital/#CorporateFinance.wacc","page":"Cost of capital","text":"wacc(; ke::Real, kd::Real, tax::Real, E::Real, D::Real)\n\nCalculates the Weighted Average Cost of Capital (WACC).\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nke: Cost of Equity.\nkd: Cost of Debt.\ntax: Corporate tax rate, between 0 and 1.\nE: Market value of equity.\nD: Market value of debt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorporateFinance.wacc"},{"category":"function","location":"cost_of_capital/#CorporateFinance.beta_regression","page":"Cost of capital","text":"beta_regression(asset_returns::AbstractVector{<:Real}, market_returns::AbstractVector{<:Real})\n\nCalculates the beta of an asset relative to the market: cov(asset_returns, market_returns) / var(market_returns).\n\nArguments\n\nasset_returns: Periodic returns of the asset.\nmarket_returns: Periodic returns of the market index, same length and periods as asset_returns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorporateFinance.beta_regression"},{"category":"function","location":"cost_of_capital/#CorporateFinance.unlever_beta","page":"Cost of capital","text":"unlever_beta(; beta::Real, tax::Real, E::Real, D::Real, beta_debt::Real = 0)\n\nRemoves the effect of debt from a levered beta: (beta + beta_debt * k) / (1 + k), with k = (1 - tax) * D / E.\n\nWith beta_debt = 0 this is the Hamada equation. A positive beta_debt (Conine formula) is more realistic for highly leveraged companies, whose debt carries part of the business risk.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nbeta: Levered (observed) beta.\ntax: Corporate tax rate, between 0 and 1.\nE: Market value of equity.\nD: Market value of debt.\nbeta_debt: Beta of the debt. Defaults to 0 (risk-free debt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorporateFinance.unlever_beta"},{"category":"function","location":"cost_of_capital/#CorporateFinance.relever_beta","page":"Cost of capital","text":"relever_beta(; beta::Real, tax::Real, E::Real, D::Real, beta_debt::Real = 0)\n\nApplies a target capital structure to an unlevered beta: beta + (beta - beta_debt) * k, with k = (1 - tax) * D / E. It is the inverse of unlever_beta.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nbeta: Unlevered beta.\ntax: Corporate tax rate, between 0 and 1.\nE: Target value of equity.\nD: Target value of debt.\nbeta_debt: Beta of the debt. Defaults to 0 (risk-free debt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CorporateFinance.relever_beta"},{"category":"section","location":"#CorporateFinance.jl","page":"Home","text":"A Julia package for corporate valuation: from the cost of capital to the equity value of a company.\n\nStep Functions\nCost of capital capm, wacc, beta_regression, unlever_beta, relever_beta\nValuation perpetuity, terminal_value, fade_forecast, dcf_valuation, dcf_sensitivity, equity_value","title":"CorporateFinance.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/ferrerama/CorporateFinance.jl\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Example:-from-beta-to-equity-value","page":"Home","text":"The example below runs when the documentation is built, so the results shown are produced by the package itself.\n\nusing CorporateFinance\n\n# Beta: unlever a comparable company, relever to the target structure\nbu = unlever_beta(beta = 1.3, tax = 0.30, E = 500_000, D = 500_000)\nbl = relever_beta(beta = bu, tax = 0.30, E = 800_000, D = 200_000)\n0.8985294117647059\n\n# Cost of equity (CAPM), including a country risk premium\nke = capm(rf = 0.04, beta = bl, erp = 0.055, crp = 0.02)\n0.10941911764705883\n\n# Weighted Average Cost of Capital\nw = wacc(ke = ke, kd = 0.06, tax = 0.30, E = 800_000, D = 200_000)\n0.09593529411764708\n\n# DCF: forecast free cash flows + terminal value with 2% growth\nfcf = [120_000, 135_000, 150_000, 160_000, 170_000]\nr = dcf_valuation(fcf; wacc = w, g = 0.02)\n(pv_fcf = 554291.8719663033, pv_terminal = 1.4443779393026708e6, enterprise_value = 1.998669811268974e6)\n\n# Sensitivity table: rows follow waccs, columns follow gs\ndcf_sensitivity(fcf; waccs = [0.09, 0.10, 0.11], gs = [0.015, 0.02, 0.025])\n3×3 Matrix{Float64}:\n 2.05866e6  2.17336e6  2.3057e6\n 1.80867e6  1.89404e6  1.9908e6\n 1.61153e6  1.67702e6  1.75021e6\n\n# Equity value\nequity_value(ev = r.enterprise_value, debt = 200_000, cash = 30_000)\n1.828669811268974e6","title":"Example: from beta to equity value"},{"category":"section","location":"#Not-covered-by-this-package","page":"Home","text":"Present value, NPV, IRR and payback are already provided by FinanceCore.jl (pv, irr) and ActuaryUtilities.jl (breakeven).","title":"Not covered by this package"}]
